336	    <section id="audit">
337	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">04</span><h2>The audit</h2></div>
338	      <p class="lede">A dependency bug found while reviewing an external PR (napi-rs publish ordering) raised the question: what else is wrong in here? Ran an adversarial sweep across the publish pipeline — bugs, performance, corner cases, DX, security — then triple-verified every finding before touching code.</p>
339	
340	      <div class="funnel">
341	        <div class="funnel-row"><span class="funnel-label">Flagged</span><div class="funnel-bar-track"><div class="funnel-bar" style="width:100%"></div></div><span class="funnel-n">18</span></div>
342	        <div class="funnel-row"><span class="funnel-label">Survived 3×</span><div class="funnel-bar-track"><div class="funnel-bar" style="width:83%"></div></div><span class="funnel-n">15</span></div>
343	        <div class="funnel-row"><span class="funnel-label">Clustered</span><div class="funnel-bar-track"><div class="funnel-bar" style="width:44%"></div></div><span class="funnel-n">8</span></div>
344	        <div class="funnel-row"><span class="funnel-label">Shipped</span><div class="funnel-bar-track"><div class="funnel-bar" style="width:33%"></div></div><span class="funnel-n">6</span></div>
345	      </div>
346	
347	      <div class="stat-strip">
348	        <div class="stat"><span class="n">64+55</span><span class="lbl">agents (2 audit rounds)</span></div>
349	        <div class="stat"><span class="n">2</span><span class="lbl">critical</span></div>
350	        <div class="stat"><span class="n">7</span><span class="lbl">high</span></div>
351	        <div class="stat"><span class="n">3</span><span class="lbl">medium</span></div>
352	        <div class="stat"><span class="n">2</span><span class="lbl">low</span></div>
353	      </div>
354	      <p style="margin-top:14px; font-size:13.5px;">Method: independent finders per dimension → adversarial skeptics per finding → a design pass weighing architecture/perf/semantics → a challenge pass hunting for sequencing conflicts before any code was written.</p>
355	    </section>
356	
357	    <section id="shipped">
358	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">05</span><h2>What shipped</h2></div>
359	      <p class="lede">Eight PRs, mostly stacked — each one branches off the last so nothing gets rebased twice. Ordered by what actually landed.</p>
360	
361	      <div class="diagram">
362	        <pre class="mermaid">
363	%%{init: {'theme':'base', 'themeVariables': {'primaryColor':'#e4eeed','primaryTextColor':'#163336','primaryBorderColor':'#2b5d62','lineColor':'#8a9092','fontFamily':'IBM Plex Mono','fontSize':'12px'}}}%%
364	graph LR
365	  main["main"] --> pr23["#23 npm gating"]
366	  pr23 --> pr24["#24 package filtering"]
367	  pr24 --> pr25["#25 dev-cycle order"]
368	  pr25 --> pr27["#27 precheck flag"]
369	  pr27 --> pr29["#29 filter-plan primitives"]
370	  main --> pr22["#22 crates.io fallback"]
371	  pr29 --> pr30["#30 jq · redaction · zip"]
372	  pr30 --> pr31["#31 tag/release rewrite"]
373	        </pre>
374	      </div>
375	
376	      <div class="pr-card">
377	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#22</span>crates.io install fallback</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
378	        <div class="pr-body">Real incident: <code>setup-callisto</code> never tried crates.io before falling to a broken git-source install. Added the missing fallback tier.</div>
379	      </div>
380	
381	      <div class="pr-card">
382	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#23</span>npm platform-dependency gating</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
383	        <div class="pr-body">A main npm package could publish even when its platform-specific sibling (the actual native binary) failed — shipping <code>optionalDependencies</code> pointing at nothing. Scoped the fix to the npm ecosystem so a same-named Cargo crate can't false-positive-match.</div>
384	      </div>
385	
386	      <div class="pr-card">
387	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#24</span>ecosystem-aware <code>--package</code> filtering</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
388	        <div class="pr-body"><b>3 findings, 1 root cause:</b> nothing cross-checked what <code>--package</code> selected against what actually landed in the plan. Fixed name collisions across ecosystems, validated platform dependencies against the final (filtered) plan, and split one misleading error into three precise ones.</div>
389	      </div>
390	
391	      <div class="pr-card">
392	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#25</span>scoped dev-cycle exclusion</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
393	        <div class="pr-body">One legitimate dev-only dependency cycle (two crates dev-depending on each other for cross-tests) silently disabled dev-ordering for <i>every</i> package in the batch. Rewrote the exclusion to Tarjan-SCC scope — only the cyclic pair loses ordering, nothing else.</div>
394	      </div>
395	
396	      <div class="pr-card">
397	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#27</span><code>--skip-publish-precheck</code></div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
398	        <div class="pr-body">Every npm publish paid for a redundant "already published?" round-trip. Made it optional — default behavior unchanged, opt in to skip it.</div>
399	      </div>
400	
401	      <div class="pr-card">
402	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#29</span><code>filter-plan</code> + primitives</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
403	        <div class="pr-body">The Rust-side foundation everything else needed: <code>PublishPlan::is_empty()</code>, <code>CreatedTag.isFloatingMajor</code>, and a new <code>filter_plan_by_report</code> function exposed as <code>callisto filter-plan</code>. No behavior change on its own — pure plumbing for #31.</div>
404	      </div>
405	
406	      <div class="pr-card">
407	        <div class="pr-head"><div class="pr-title"><span class="pr-num">#30</span>jq consolidation · log redaction · Windows zip</div><span class="status-chip merged">merged</span></div>
408	        <div class="pr-body">Three independent, low-risk fixes bundled in one PR — built by three parallel agents on disjoint files. Native-artifact loop went from 1+2N to 1 <code>jq</code> spawn; credential redaction now covers the <i>live</i> CI log stream, not just the final error; Windows binary installs now actually extract (was silently broken, masked by an unrelated fallback).</div>
409	      </div>
410	    </section>
411	
412	    <section id="bigfix">
413	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">06</span><h2>The big fix — #31</h2></div>
414	      <p class="lede">Stacked last on purpose: it's the same file class that caused a real production incident earlier in this effort (the original floating-tag force-push bug). Three findings, one root cause — the shell layer had no reliable way to know <i>which packages actually published</i> or <i>which tags are floating vs. immutable</i>. <a href="https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/pull/31">Currently open, flagged for close review.</a></p>
415	
416	      <h3 style="font-size:16px; margin-top:28px;">Force-push scope</h3>
417	      <div class="compare">
418	        <div class="compare-panel before">
419	          <div class="compare-head">before</div>
420	          <div class="compare-body">callisto tag --plan "$PLAN_JSON" \
421	  --floating-major || true
422	git push origin --tags --force</div>
423	        </div>
424	        <div class="compare-panel after">
425	          <div class="compare-head">after</div>
426	          <div class="compare-body">TAG_REPORT_JSON=$(callisto tag ...)
427	# isFloatingMajor splits the set
428	git push origin "${IMMUTABLE[@]}"
429	git push origin --force "${FLOATING[@]}"</div>
430	        </div>
431	      </div>
432	      <p style="font-size:13.5px; margin-top:8px;">The blanket force-push clobbered immutable per-version release tags along with the one alias meant to move. <code>|| true</code> hid a real <code>callisto tag</code> failure entirely.</p>
433	
434	      <h3 style="font-size:16px; margin-top:28px;">Empty-plan gating</h3>
435	      <ol class="step-list">
436	        <li><b>Before:</b> a docs-only commit after a release still ran artifact placement, publish, tag, and release — reporting <code>published=true</code> for a run that shipped nothing.</li>
437	        <li><b>After:</b> plan computed once, checked against <code>PublishPlan::is_empty()</code> semantics, everything downstream gated on it.</li>
438	      </ol>
439	
440	      <h3 style="font-size:16px; margin-top:28px;">Partial-failure survivors</h3>
441	      <ol class="step-list">
442	        <li><b>Before:</b> one package's publish failure killed the step under <code>set -e</code> — already-succeeded siblings never got tagged or released, on every retry, until the failure was fixed.</li>
443	        <li><b>After:</b> exit code captured, <code>filter-plan</code> narrows the plan to confirmed successes, tag/release run against <i>that</i>, job fails afterward for the real failure.</li>
444	      </ol>
445	
446	      <div class="risk-note">Flagged for review, not a quick merge: same file class as the earlier production incident. 8 shell test files, 36 assertions, all green — but shell-script correctness under real GitHub Actions conditions is harder to fully simulate than Rust.</div>
447	    </section>
448	
