257	    <header class="head">
258	      <div class="eyebrow">callisto · release engineering briefing</div>
259	      <h1 class="title">How Callisto Ships Software</h1>
260	      <p class="dek">What callisto is, how it's built, and what changed across the last ten pull requests — an adversarial audit of the publish pipeline and the fixes that came out of it.</p>
261	    </header>
262	
263	    <section id="positioning">
264	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">01</span><h2>What callisto is</h2></div>
265	      <p class="lede">Callisto is a release-automation CLI for polyglot monorepos: a contributor describes the impact of their change in plain language, callisto turns that into version bumps, changelogs, git tags, and registry publishes — for Rust, npm, and Python packages in the same repo, versioned and released together.</p>
266	      <p>It follows the <b>changesets</b> model (popularized by the JavaScript ecosystem's <code>@changesets/cli</code>): instead of inferring intent from commit messages, a contributor runs <code>callisto add</code> and writes one sentence plus a severity (patch/minor/major) per affected package. That file — a changeset — lives in the repo until a release, so version decisions are explicit, reviewable in a PR, and never guessed from regex-matching commit prefixes.</p>
267	
268	      <p style="margin-top:28px; font-size:15px; font-weight:600; color:var(--ink);">Release engineering tools all answer the same question — given a pile of merged PRs, what ships, at what version, to which registry? — but differ in two axes: how they capture intent, and how many ecosystems they actually understand at once.</p>
269	
270	      <div class="table-wrap">
271	        <table>
272	          <thead>
273	            <tr><th>Capability</th><th class="callisto-col">callisto</th><th>@changesets/cli</th><th>release-please</th><th>knope</th></tr>
274	          </thead>
275	          <tbody>
276	            <tr><td>Intent format</td><td class="callisto-col">Changesets (byte-compat)</td><td>Changesets</td><td>Conventional Commits</td><td>Changesets or commits</td></tr>
277	            <tr><td>Cargo workspaces</td><td class="callisto-col">✓ native</td><td class="no">✗</td><td>✓ plugin</td><td>✓</td></tr>
278	            <tr><td>npm workspaces</td><td class="callisto-col">✓</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr>
279	            <tr><td>Cross-ecosystem cascade</td><td class="callisto-col">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td><td>✓ per-ecosystem</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
280	            <tr><td>napi platform-package coordination</td><td class="callisto-col">✓ native</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
281	            <tr><td>GitHub Release binary assets</td><td class="callisto-col">planned</td><td class="no">✗</td><td>✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
282	          </tbody>
283	        </table>
284	      </div>
285	      <p style="margin-top:12px; font-size:14px;"><b>Cross-ecosystem cascade</b> means a version bump propagates along real dependency edges — if a Cargo crate bumps and an npm package depends on it, the npm package bumps too, automatically. <b>napi platform-package coordination</b> is the sharper case: one native Rust crate compiles to N architecture-specific npm packages plus one JS wrapper package that depends on all of them — nothing else in this table treats that shape as a first-class case instead of a hand-rolled CI workaround.</p>
286	      <p style="margin-top:10px; font-size:14px;">The niche: teams who like changesets' explicit-intent model but ship a real polyglot monorepo — Rust crates, npm packages, napi native bindings, PyPI wheels — under one version plan.</p>
287	    </section>
288	
289	    <section id="concepts">
290	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">01.5</span><h2>Five terms worth knowing</h2></div>
291	      <p class="lede">These recur through the rest of this report — worth pinning down once rather than re-explaining inline each time.</p>
292	      <div class="callout">
293	        <dl>
294	          <dt>changeset</dt>
295	          <dd>A markdown file (<code>.changeset/*.md</code>) recording one contributor's intent: which packages changed, at what severity, in one sentence. Consumed and deleted when <code>callisto version</code> runs.</dd>
296	          <dt>cascade</dt>
297	          <dd>Propagating a version bump along dependency edges: if <code>core</code> bumps minor and <code>web-app</code> depends on <code>core</code>, <code>web-app</code> gets a patch bump too — even though nobody wrote a changeset for <code>web-app</code>.</dd>
298	          <dt>fixed / linked group</dt>
299	          <dd>A set of packages callisto forces to share one version number (fixed) or move in lockstep by severity without necessarily matching digits (linked) — for packages that are conceptually "one thing" split across files.</dd>
300	          <dt>platform package</dt>
301	          <dd>An architecture-specific npm package generated by a native build (napi-rs or maturin) — e.g. <code>my-lib-darwin-arm64</code>. Never hand-published; always a dependency of one "main" wrapper package.</dd>
302	          <dt>floating major tag</dt>
303	          <dd>A git tag like <code>v1</code> that's meant to move — re-pointed at every new v1.x.y release, unlike an immutable per-version tag such as <code>v1.2.3</code>, which is created once and must never move again.</dd>
304	        </dl>
305	      </div>
306	    </section>
307	
308	    <section id="architecture">
309	      <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">02</span><h2>Crate architecture</h2></div>
310	      <p class="lede">Ten Rust crates in three layers, workspace-wide <code>unsafe_code = "forbid"</code>. The split isn't arbitrary — it's a licensing and reuse boundary: layer 1 stays permissively licensed (MIT/Apache-2.0) and free of any dependency on the rest, so it can be vendored into someone else's tool without dragging callisto's own engine along.</p>
311	
312	      <div class="callout" style="margin-top:20px;">
313	        <p><b>Layer 1 — core types.</b> <code>callisto-model</code> defines the vocabulary every other crate shares: <code>PackageId</code>, <code>Version</code>, the JSON report shapes. <code>callisto-format</code> parses and writes changeset markdown as a lossless CST — editing a changeset preserves the contributor's original formatting instead of round-tripping through a generic YAML/markdown serializer.</p>
314	        <p><b>Layer 2 — the engine.</b> <code>callisto-graph</code> is where the actual logic lives: building the dependency graph, computing cascades, planning what publishes, orchestrating registry calls. It leans on <code>callisto-vcs</code> for git access, <code>callisto-manifests</code> for format-preserving edits to <code>Cargo.toml</code>/<code>package.json</code>/<code>pyproject.toml</code>, plus <code>callisto-changelog</code> and <code>callisto-conventional</code> for changelog generation and optional Conventional-Commits inference.</p>
315	        <p><b>Layer 3 — what you actually run.</b> <code>callisto-cli</code> is the binary every command in this report goes through. <code>callisto-moon</code> is a plugin exposing the same engine inside <a href="https://moonrepo.dev">moon</a>'s build-orchestration graph, compiled to WASI.</p>
316	      </div>
