    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "A newly created release tag is annotated with the source commit SHA and is created at that exact commit. If an expected tag already exists, the command resolves its actual target and reports either an idempotent match or a conflict; it never silently reports the requested SHA for a differently targeted tag.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The documented baseline policy is SHA-anchored provenance: plans, tags, artifacts, and reports identify the same immutable commit. Documentation explicitly says this binds state but does not prove human authorship. Optional signature verification is represented as a policy capability with named trust roots and failure behavior, not hard-wired into the core model.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Provider adapters may add attestations or signature-verification evidence, but their absence cannot cause the core domain crates to depend on a forge SDK. Adapter evidence records issuer, subject, verification time, and the bound source SHA so consumers can audit it.",
