{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001",
  "purpose": "Define an optional, durable release-plan contract that lets a reviewed versioning decision be handed from planning to execution without treating a CI cache or an untracked workspace file as authoritative state.",
  "scope": "A versioned JSON VersionPlan and PublishPlan envelope, their CLI serialization and validation boundaries, and GitHub Actions artifact handoff within the existing single-job callisto-action workflow. The core model is forge-neutral; the GitHub workflow is only one adapter.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Implementing a distributed transaction across registries, Git, and a forge.",
    "Committing transient plan files, CI outputs, or caches to the repository.",
    "Using a GitHub Actions cache as a correctness or recovery mechanism.",
    "Replacing committed manifests, changelogs, release tags, or registry state as the durable records of a completed release.",
    "Restructuring .github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml into separate planning and execution jobs. The plan/execute handoff this spec defines operates within the existing single job, single shell block (plan-publish followed by publish in the same step sequence); a plain plan file written to disk by plan-publish and read back by publish within that same job satisfies the durability and validation requirements below. Uploading the plan as a workflow artifact (AC-003) is for post-hoc inspection and audit, not for crossing a job boundary that does not exist."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "The serialized envelope has an explicit schema_version, source_commit, a digest_scheme_version, a config_digest, an input_digest, a generation timestamp, and a payload containing the planned package versions and ordered operations. The digest_scheme_version is incremented whenever the set of hashed fields or the canonicalization procedure changes, independently of schema_version, so a future config-shape change cannot silently produce a mismatched digest that wrongly accepts or rejects a plan. Both digests use SHA-256 over a canonical encoding: object keys sorted lexicographically at every nesting level, no insignificant whitespace, floating-point values (none expected in current config/manifest shapes) formatted per ECMA-404 shortest round-trip representation, and fields whose value is absent (Rust None) omitted from the canonical form identically to a field that was never present in the source data (i.e. an explicit null is never emitted by the canonicalizer). config_digest hashes only the release-relevant configuration file(s) (callisto workspace/package config, e.g. Cargo.toml/package.json/pyproject.toml [publish] metadata as consumed by plan-publish) as read from disk before planning begins. input_digest hashes the resolved workspace/package manifest state (package names, current versions, dependency edges) as read from disk before planning begins. Neither digest is computed over the PublishPlan envelope itself or any of its own fields, avoiding self-reference. Unknown future schema_version or digest_scheme_version values are rejected before execution with an actionable diagnostic naming the unsupported version and the highest version this build supports.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Execution accepts an explicit plan path or stdin and validates the envelope before any mutation. Validation is a total, all-fields comparison against freshly observed state, covering every field enumerated in AC-001 (schema_version, digest_scheme_version, source_commit, config_digest, input_digest) plus the full requested operation set (every entry in rust_crates, npm_platform_packages, npm_main_packages, pypi_packages, and releases) — an omitted field, a field newly defaulted since the plan was generated, or an operation present in one side and absent in the other counts as a mismatch, never as equality. The comparison fails closed: any error while recomputing current state (unreadable file, unparseable manifest, digest computation failure) is treated as a mismatch and blocks execution, never as a pass. On any mismatch or comparison error, execution fails before mutation and the diagnostic identifies every mismatching or unrecomputable field, not just the first one found.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Plans are emitted only on an explicit request. Local default commands leave no plan file behind. Within the existing single-job GitHub Actions workflow, plan-publish writes the plan to an explicit file path in the job's own workspace and the subsequent publish step in that same job reads it back from that same path — no CI cache, no cross-job artifact download, and no implicit discovery by searching the workspace for a plan file with an unspecified name. Independently of that same-job handoff, CI also uploads the explicitly generated plan as an immutable workflow artifact with a bounded retention period, keyed to the workflow run, for post-hoc inspection and audit; nothing in this spec requires a downstream job to consume that uploaded artifact, since no downstream job exists.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The plan is structurally guaranteed to be secret-free by construction: every field on VersionPlan, PublishPlan, and its nested types (CratePublish, NpmPublish, NpmMainPublish, PypiPublish, ReleaseEntry) is populated only from repository content (Cargo.toml/package.json/pyproject.toml metadata, git refs, computed versions) or static configuration, never from a process environment variable, a CLI credential flag, or any value that flows through the registry-authentication path. A test asserts this structurally: it enumerates the fields on each plan/envelope type and confirms each is sourced from a named repo-content or config accessor, not from std::env or an equivalent credential-reading path; a new field added to any of these types without a corresponding accessor-provenance entry fails the test. This replaces reliance on a runtime string/entropy scan of serialized output, which cannot prove absence and is not required by this criterion.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The plan/report distinguishes planned, attempted, completed, skipped, and failed operations. The default behavior for every invocation, with or without a durable plan present, remains recomputing the release plan fresh from repository, tag, and registry observations (the existing plan-publish behavior) — this recompute-from-source-of-truth path is never removed or bypassed by default. When a durable plan artifact from a prior interrupted execution is explicitly supplied, a new invocation may use it as a fast-path optimization to produce a reconciliation report without repeating the full observation pass, but only after validating it per AC-002; if validation fails, or no plan is supplied, execution falls back to the full recompute path with no degradation in correctness or safety.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY"
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002",
  "purpose": "Bind release intent, release tags, and publish reports to one immutable source commit while keeping the core release engine independent of GitHub or any particular signing service.",
  "scope": "Source-commit resolution and validation rules layered on top of the plan-level `source_commit` field already mandated by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001 AC-001; per-package `ReleaseEntry.sha` consistency with that field; commit and tag resolution before mutation; provider-neutral provenance policy; clear diagnostics for mismatch. Forge-specific authorization and attestation integration are adapters. Note: the execution-time HEAD == source_commit check specified in AC-002 is currently near-tautological in the existing single-job CI architecture (one checkout, no intervening git mutation between planning and execution). It becomes load-bearing once SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001's plan/execute job split ships, at which point the executing job must consume a pinned-SHA checkout of source_commit (not a floating branch/ref). This spec has an explicit ordering dependency on that job-split work landing correctly.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Mandating GPG, SSH, Sigstore, GitHub, GitLab, Bazel, or any other forge or signing provider.",
    "Claiming that a Git SHA is a cryptographic author signature.",
    "Changing a pre-existing tag to force it onto a requested commit.",
    "Wiring the SignaturePolicy type introduced in AC-004 into any specific signing or attestation provider — this spec requires only that the policy surface exist and be representable."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "This spec does not introduce the plan-level `source_commit` field -- that field is defined by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001 AC-001, which mandates it on the plan envelope. This spec adds resolution and validation rules on top of that field: `source_commit` is resolved from Git at planning time and is never inferred from a branch name during execution; every generated VersionPlan, PublishPlan, tag report, and publish report carries the full resolved SHA (not a shortened form) via that field. Additionally, for every `ReleaseEntry` in a plan, its existing `sha: CommitSha` field (crates/callisto-model/src/plan.rs) must equal the plan-level `source_commit`; plan validation rejects a plan where any entry's `sha` diverges from the plan-level `source_commit`, reporting the entry's package id, its sha, and the plan's source_commit in the diagnostic.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Before any apply, tag, or publish mutation, execution resolves HEAD and requires it to equal the plan's `source_commit`. A detached HEAD is permitted when it matches; a branch name, shallow symbolic reference, or changed worktree cannot substitute for equality. The comparison is an exact, full-length, case-normalized (lowercase hex) string comparison of the complete SHA -- a prefix or short-SHA match is never treated as equality, even when the prefix is unambiguous, to prevent a false pass on hash-prefix coincidence. On mismatch, execution fails before any mutation and reports a stable, structured error type/variant (not free-text-only diagnostic text) carrying both the resolved HEAD SHA and the plan's source_commit, so calling code -- including the GitHub Actions adapter per SPEC-GITHUB-RELEASE-HARDENING-003 AC-004 -- can match on the error type programmatically without string-parsing.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "A newly created release tag is an annotated tag (`git tag -a`, i.e. a Git tag object, not a lightweight ref) created at the exact source commit SHA and carrying that SHA as its target. This is a new invariant layered on top of the already-shipped idempotency behavior of `CreatedTag` (crates/callisto-model/src/report.rs): the existing `sha` and `already_existed` fields, and the existing idempotent-match-vs-conflict semantics documented at docs/01-spec.md §M.12.6, are unchanged by this spec and are not re-specified here.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The documented baseline policy is SHA-anchored provenance: plans, tags, artifacts, and reports identify the same immutable commit. Documentation explicitly says this binds state but does not prove human authorship. Optional signature verification is represented as a policy capability with a concrete minimum shape: a `SignaturePolicy` type carrying `trust_roots: Vec<TrustRoot>` (opaque, provider-defined identifiers) and `on_missing: SignaturePolicyAction` where `SignaturePolicyAction` is one of `Reject | Warn | Ignore`. This spec requires only that the `SignaturePolicy` type and its policy surface exist and be representable in the core model; it does not require wiring it into any specific signing or attestation provider (see non_goals).",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Provider adapters may add attestations or signature-verification evidence, but their absence cannot cause the core domain crates to depend on a forge SDK. When present, adapter evidence (issuer, subject, verification time, and the bound source SHA) is stored as a field on the tag report or publish report (the same reports already carrying `CreatedTag`/publish results), so a consumer reading that report can audit the evidence without a forge-specific integration.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE"
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-GITHUB-RELEASE-HARDENING-003",
  "purpose": "Harden the GitHub Actions release adapter so registry publication and repository mutation occur only through a narrowly authorized, reviewable release boundary.",
  "scope": ".github/workflows/callisto-release.yml and its referenced actions, GitHub Environment configuration documentation, action pinning, permissions, artifact handoff, and workflow tests. The non_goal below of not making GitHub Actions the only supported CI/release platform refers to the portability of the callisto CLI as a build tool; it does not constrain the mechanisms this repository's own meta-release pipeline may use. A GitHub-Environment-specific protection gate on this repo's release workflow (AC-001) is in scope and does not conflict with that non-goal. AC-006 (durable-plan artifact handoff) has an undeclared build dependency on SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001: that spec's artifact schema and upload/download flow do not exist yet in the current workflow, where the plan is computed inline with no handoff. This spec's AC-006 must be sequenced and implemented after SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001 ships.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Making GitHub Actions the only supported CI or release platform.",
    "Moving provider-neutral release rules from callisto core into workflow shell code.",
    "Requiring Git tag signing in this track; signature policy is covered by SPEC-RELEASE-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "The workflow's current single `release` job (which today computes the plan and performs tag/publish/gh-release in one shell script) is split into two jobs: an unprivileged planning job with no registry secrets and a read-only or no GITHUB_TOKEN, and a separate privileged execution job that performs tag/publish/release actions. The execution job is bound to a GitHub Environment (`environment:` key referencing a named environment such as `release`) that has required reviewers and branch/tag deployment rules configured. Non-release jobs (verify, planning) receive neither registry secrets nor write-scoped GITHUB_TOKEN permissions.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Each workflow and job declares the least GitHub token permissions it needs. contents, pull-requests, checks, and statuses are write-scoped only in the job that demonstrably performs that operation; all other jobs default to read or none. A workflow test or policy check rejects an unscoped top-level write grant.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Third-party GitHub Actions are pinned to immutable full commit SHAs (40 hex characters), each with a nearby comment recording the upstream tag or release used, for human review.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004b",
      "criterion": "CI verifies every external action reference's pin format and fails the build if any external action reference is changed to a mutable tag or branch instead of a full commit SHA.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "A CI or setup check calls the GitHub API (e.g. `gh api repos/{repo}/environments/{name}`) against the environment named in AC-001's `environment:` key and fails if that environment's `protection_rules` array is empty. A workflow diff that merely references `environment: release` by name is not sufficient, since GitHub auto-creates a referenced environment with zero protection rules on first use unless required reviewers have been separately configured via repo Settings.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "The planning job (per AC-001) uploads the explicit durable plan defined by SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001 as a build artifact. The privileged execution job downloads only that artifact, verifies its source_commit against its own checkout, and fails before any registry or repository credentials are used if validation fails. Caches may accelerate dependency installation but are never read as release intent. Implementation of this criterion is blocked on SPEC-RELEASE-PLAN-DURABILITY-001 shipping its artifact schema and upload/download flow first; see scope note on sequencing.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Changes to files under `.github/workflows/` and `.github/actions/` require review from a designated set of owners before merge, enforced via CODEOWNERS combined with a branch protection rule requiring that review on those paths. This is distinct from and in addition to AC-001's environment gate: the environment gate controls who can trigger a *deployment* from an already-merged workflow, while this criterion controls who can *merge changes* to the workflow/action definitions that determine what a future deployment does.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-GITHUB-RELEASE-HARDENING"
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-SUPPLY-CHAIN-REPRODUCIBILITY-004",
  "purpose": "Make dependency-security results reproducible, auditable, and actionable, including the current transitive yanked bisync finding and advisory exceptions.",
  "scope": "Cargo audit/deny configuration and lockfile policy, advisory database update behavior, exception records, and remediation tracking. This is a supply-chain control track, not a license-policy redesign.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Promising an immediate upstream replacement for every transitive dependency.",
    "Suppressing a finding merely because it is transitive or development-only.",
    "Adding runtime telemetry or network access to production binaries."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Security CI records, for each result, the advisory database revision and the tool (cargo-deny) version used, and its behavior is deterministic enough that a maintainer can reproduce a pass or failure from the recorded inputs. cargo-deny itself has no built-in flag or JSON field that reports the advisory-db revision (verified against `cargo deny check --help`, `cargo deny fetch --help`, and actual JSON output): recording it requires an additional step outside cargo-deny, such as a CI step running `git -C <advisory-db-path> rev-parse HEAD` against the cloned `~/.cargo/advisory-dbs/advisory-db-<hash>/` directory, or pinning `db-urls`/a fixed db commit in deny.toml. This is a workaround around a cargo-deny gap, not a native capability, and must be implemented as such rather than assumed to exist as a flag.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "When the advisory database update or a network fetch fails, CI reports this distinctly from a clean audit result (i.e., a fetch/update failure is never conflated with or silently treated as an audit pass, nor reported using the same status/message as a genuine clean-audit outcome).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Every active advisory, yanked-package, or license exception has a structured justification naming the affected dependency path, owner or tracking issue, creation date, and expiry/review date. The expiry/review date must not exceed 90 days from creation (or last review); an exception past this window is not compliant regardless of its literal expiry field value, which enforces the 'bounded' requirement in AC-005 rather than leaving it as an unbounded free-text date.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "CI fails when an exception's expiry/review date has passed, or when its recorded dependency path no longer matches the lockfile. A dependency path 'no longer matches' both when the named path is absent from the lockfile entirely and when the same crate is still present but reachable only through a different intermediate dependency chain (e.g., after `cargo update` reroutes it through a new intermediate crate) — in the rerouted case CI must also fail, since the exception's recorded path no longer reflects the actual resolution and requires re-justification against the new path.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The transitive bisync yanked-package finding is resolved by a compatible dependency update or retained only under an explicit bounded exception meeting AC-003 and AC-004. This finding reaches production code (callisto-cli/callisto-graph -> callisto-vcs -> gix -> gix-protocol -> bisync), unlike the 11 pre-existing ignored advisories in deny.toml, which are all dev-only (via moon_pdk_test_utils/warpgate/wasmtime) — the production-code reachability means this exception cannot be granted the same casual treatment as those dev-only entries and requires a materially stronger compensating control or owner sign-off. The release documentation identifies the chosen state; an untracked yanked package cannot remain a silent warning.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "The audit command used locally and in CI is documented as one supported task-runner entry point and emits machine-readable results suitable for artifact retention. Keeping secret values out of logs and artifacts is a forward-looking guard for a future private-registry or token-authenticated scenario, not a testable property of the current system: today's audit path (public advisory-db, public crates.io index) handles no credentials, so this clause is not falsifiable against present behavior and must not be treated as a current-system gap.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-SUPPLY-CHAIN-REPRODUCIBILITY"
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-DOCUMENTATION-AUTHORITY-005",
  "purpose": "Keep product specifications, semantic models, implementation plans, and historical handoffs understandable to future contributors and agents by assigning each a precise authority and correcting confirmed current-state drift.",
  "scope": "Documentation authority policy, status metadata, link routing, and the confirmed stale claims in design and semantic-model documents. This track establishes maintenance rules without rewriting repository history.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Deleting historical handoffs, plans, or Git history.",
    "Claiming that a plan is normative product behavior after code and specification have changed.",
    "Creating a second prose copy of every normative requirement."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "A documentation authority index file exists at docs/AUTHORITY.md. It states which document classes are normative (docs/01-spec.md and accepted .claude/specs/*/spec@1 acceptance criteria), which explain rationale (docs/00-design.md), which describe verified current implementation (.claude/semantic-model/*.md), which describe intended work and status (.claude/plans/*.md), and which are historical context only (handoff documents). The index states one concrete resolution rule for conflicts: the normative source (docs/01-spec.md or an accepted spec) always wins; a non-normative document found to conflict with it receives a dated correction note in place pointing to the normative source, never a silent rewrite of its own original claims.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Every spec@1 that is newly created or materially revised includes a status field (draft, active, or superseded), a last_verified field naming the source commit or revision it was checked against, an owner/track field, and a link to its implementation plan. 'Materially revised' means any change to acceptance_criteria, scope, or non_goals; a wording-only or formatting-only change is not material and does not require updating these fields. Completed or superseded plans include a header line linking back to the normative requirement or spec they implement, rather than presenting their own content as current truth.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Confirmed stale current-state claims are corrected in the authoritative/current-description location: the design document's shipped-language posture (docs/00-design.md line 3 vs. §17 Milestones), the semantic version-flow changeset staging behavior (.claude/semantic-model/version-flow.md lines 78-85 vs. crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs:275-286), and the semantic error-taxonomy PackageSetMatchedNothing emission behavior (.claude/semantic-model/error-taxonomy.md vs. crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs:425). Each correction is validated against the referenced implementation and does not erase historical rationale.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "A documentation authority check, runnable via `just lint-docs`, verifies that every file under docs/*.md and .claude/semantic-model/*.md carries a required status/authority frontmatter field (e.g. `authority: normative|rationale|verified-current`), detected by presence-of-field, not free-text tense matching. The check lists any such file missing the field and reports it without automatically rewriting file content.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The `just lint-docs` check does not scan or flag files under .claude/plans/ or any handoff directory for the authority frontmatter field, consistent with non_goals excluding plans and handoffs from normative rewriting. A plan or handoff document missing the field never appears in the check's report.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-DOCUMENTATION-AUTHORITY"
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-LANE-POLICY-006",
  "purpose": "Make release-train, fixed-group, linked-group, prerelease, and stable-release interactions explicit before adding orchestration that could produce competing version authority.",
  "scope": "A normative release-lane policy and validation contract for the existing version planner. The policy covers supported combinations, precedence, and rejection diagnostics.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Implementing a full release-train scheduler or time-based calendar.",
    "Changing existing fixed- or linked-group version semantics without an approved follow-on specification.",
    "Inferring organization-specific release cadence from package names or branch names.",
    "Re-validating or duplicating the fixed/linked-group membership conflict detection already implemented in crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs (GraphError::ConflictingGroupMembership, E109)."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "The normative documentation names, for every package at a given planning point, exactly one authority responsible for selecting that package's next version: the package itself (independent lane), its fixed group, its linked group, or its prerelease lane. For each pair of these four authority kinds, the documentation states explicitly whether that pairing may apply to the same package concurrently and, if so, which authority's version decision takes precedence.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "The policy codifies the fixed/linked-group membership conflict detection already implemented in GroupTable::resolve (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs) as normative: a package listed as a member of two conflicting fixed or linked groups is rejected during config resolution via GraphError::ConflictingGroupMembership, with a diagnostic naming the package and both conflicting group names, before any planning proceeds. This criterion documents existing, already-shipped and already-tested behavior (crates/callisto-graph/tests/config_resolve_tests.rs) and requires no new detection logic.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002a",
      "criterion": "A package that is a member of a fixed or linked group and is also placed in a prerelease lane for the same planning run is rejected before manifest, tag, changelog, or registry mutation, with a diagnostic naming the package, the group, and the prerelease lane, unless the policy explicitly designates one of the two as taking precedence -- in which case the documentation states the precedence rule and a test exercises the non-precedent lane being silently subordinated rather than rejected. This is net-new: no existing code in crates/callisto-graph checks for a prerelease-lane vs. fixed/linked-group conflict.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "The policy states, for each of the following starting states of a package's prerelease lane -- no prerelease lane active, prerelease lane active and eligible for promotion, prerelease lane active and abandoned -- whether a stable-lane publish for that package is permitted, blocked, or requires an explicit promotion step, and an automated test exists for each of the three states asserting the planner's accept/reject decision. A stable-lane publish attempted from a state the policy does not list in this criterion fails planning with a diagnostic naming the package and its current prerelease-lane state, before any manifest, tag, changelog, or registry mutation occurs.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "Given identical source tree contents, configuration files, and CLI/programmatic inputs, two consecutive invocations of the planner produce byte-for-byte identical serialized ordered-operation lists and byte-for-byte identical diagnostic output (including diagnostic ordering). An automated test runs the planner twice against one fixture and asserts equality of both outputs.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-RELEASE-LANE-POLICY"
}

