<teammate-message teammate_id="draft-004b" color="red" summary="Revised SPEC-004 JSON, all 9 blockers fixed">
Revised spec below. Verified against repo: `.moon/tasks/rust.yml:44-46` (audit = `cargo-deny check advisories`), `Justfile:51-52` (`just audit` -> `moon run :audit`), `cargo deny --help` (`-f/--format` is a global flag, `json` is a valid value, confirmed on installed cargo-deny 0.19.9), `deny.toml` (11 existing dev-only ignores), `cargo tree -i bisync` (confirms callisto-cli/callisto-graph -> callisto-vcs -> gix 0.86.0 -> gix-protocol 0.64.0 -> bisync 0.3.0), and crates.io dependency manifest for gix-protocol 0.65.0/0.65.1 (latest published version) which still lists bisync as a required dependency — so no clean upstream fix exists today; AC-005 now commits to the exception-only path. docs/ is a flat directory with no existing security doc, so I named a new file `docs/security-advisories.md` matching the existing flat, unnumbered-file convention (e.g. `release-paradigms.md`).

```json
{
  "id": "SPEC-SUPPLY-CHAIN-REPRODUCIBILITY-004",
  "purpose": "Make dependency-security results reproducible, auditable, and actionable, including the current transitive yanked bisync finding and advisory exceptions.",
  "scope": "Cargo audit/deny configuration and lockfile policy, advisory database update behavior, exception records, and remediation tracking. This is a supply-chain control track, not a license-policy redesign.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Promising an immediate upstream replacement for every transitive dependency.",
    "Suppressing a finding merely because it is transitive or development-only.",
    "Adding runtime telemetry or network access to production binaries.",
    "Redaction of registry credentials or tokens from audit logs and artifacts -- no credential-bearing audit path exists today (public advisory-db, public crates.io index); this is a forward-looking guard for a future private-registry or token-authenticated scenario, not a testable property of the current system."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Security CI writes a JSON artifact to `target/security-audit-record.json` for each audit run, containing at minimum: `advisory_db_commit_id` (the 40-character hex SHA-1 of the advisory-db HEAD), `cargo_deny_version` (the cargo-deny binary version string), `cargo_lock_hash` (the SHA-256 hex digest of the audited Cargo.lock), `advisory_ids` (the array of RUSTSEC/advisory IDs from cargo-deny's own `cargo deny check advisories -f json` output), and `exit_code` (the exit code of that command). `advisory_db_commit_id` is obtained via the `rustsec` crate's `Database::latest_commit()` (`git` feature), whose returned `Commit` carries `commit_id`; this is the sole, binding recording method -- no `git rev-parse HEAD` shell-out fallback against a guessed `$CARGO_HOME/advisory-dbs/*/` path is used. Reproduction contract: given a recorded (`advisory_db_commit_id`, `cargo_deny_version`, `cargo_lock_hash`) triple, checking out the advisory-db at `advisory_db_commit_id`, running the cargo-deny binary at `cargo_deny_version`, against a Cargo.lock whose SHA-256 matches `cargo_lock_hash`, and executing `cargo deny check advisories -f json` reproduces the identical `advisory_ids` set and the identical `exit_code`.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "When the advisory database update or a network fetch fails, CI reports this distinctly from a clean audit result (i.e., a fetch/update failure is never conflated with or silently treated as an audit pass, nor reported using the same status/message as a genuine clean-audit outcome).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Every active advisory, yanked-package, or license exception has a structured justification record naming the affected dependency path, owner or tracking issue, creation date, and expiry/review date, where the expiry/review date is no more than 90 days after the creation date (or the last review date, if the exception was renewed). AC-004 enforces this 90-day ceiling and the dependency-path-currency requirement as CI failure conditions.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
