{
  "id": "SPEC-SUPPLY-CHAIN-REPRODUCIBILITY-004",
  "purpose": "Make dependency-security audit results reproducible, auditable, and actionable: every audit run is byte-reproducible from a recorded triple, fetch/database failures are never conflated with a clean pass, and every advisory/yanked/license exception is a structured, time-bounded, mechanically-checked record instead of an untracked line in deny.toml.",
  "scope": "Cargo audit/deny configuration and CI wiring, advisory-database revision recording, advisory database update/fetch failure handling, and a structured exception-record store for deny.toml ignores. This is a supply-chain control track, not a license-policy redesign.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Promising an immediate upstream replacement for every transitive dependency (e.g. the bisync/gix-protocol yanked-package finding is exception-only in this spec; no upstream fix is available as of this writing).",
    "Suppressing a finding merely because it is transitive or development-only -- AC-005 explicitly requires stronger fields for exceptions reachable from production code.",
    "Adding runtime telemetry or network access to production (non-dev, non-CI) binaries.",
    "Redaction of registry credentials or tokens from audit logs and artifacts -- no credential-bearing audit path exists today (public advisory-db, public crates.io index); this is out of scope until a private-registry or token-authenticated scenario exists.",
    "Migrating or restructuring the existing 17 dev-only wasmtime/proc-macro-error/im-rc ignores in deny.toml into the new exception-record store -- this spec's exception-record requirement (AC-003/AC-004) applies only to exceptions created or renewed after this spec ships; the pre-existing 17 remain governed by deny.toml's existing comment block until independently migrated."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "A CI-run audit tool writes a JSON artifact to `target/security-audit-record.json` for every `just audit` execution in the `security` job of `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml`, containing exactly these fields: `advisory_db_commit_id` (string, the 40-character lowercase hex SHA-1 of the advisory-db HEAD), `cargo_deny_version` (string, the `cargo-deny --version` output, e.g. `\"cargo-deny 0.19.9\"`), `cargo_lock_hash` (string, the lowercase hex SHA-256 digest of the audited Cargo.lock file's bytes), `advisory_ids` (array of strings, the RUSTSEC/advisory IDs from `cargo deny -f json check advisories`'s output, sorted ascending), and `exit_code` (integer, the exit code of that `cargo deny -f json check advisories` invocation). `advisory_db_commit_id` is obtained by calling the `rustsec` crate's (newly added, version `0.33.0`, `git` feature enabled) `Database::fetch()`, then `.latest_commit()` on the result, taking `commit_id` from the returned `Commit` -- this is the sole, binding recording method; no `git rev-parse HEAD` shell-out against a guessed `$CARGO_HOME` path is used. Reproduction contract: given a recorded (`advisory_db_commit_id`, `cargo_deny_version`, `cargo_lock_hash`) triple, checking out the advisory-db at `advisory_db_commit_id`, running the cargo-deny binary matching `cargo_deny_version`, against a Cargo.lock whose SHA-256 matches `cargo_lock_hash`, and executing `cargo deny -f json check advisories` reproduces the identical `advisory_ids` set and the identical `exit_code`.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "When `rustsec::Database::fetch()` (the same call used by AC-001) returns an `Err` -- i.e. the advisory-database fetch/update itself fails, whether from network failure or a git-protocol error -- the audit tool exits with code `3` and prints a message containing the literal substring `advisory database update failed`, followed by the underlying error. It does not write `target/security-audit-record.json`. This exit code and message are distinct from every other defined exit code in this spec (`0` clean pass, `1` advisory findings present, `2` AC-007's revision-undetermined case, `4` AC-004's exception-validation failure), so CI can never conflate a fetch/update failure with a clean audit result or report it under a clean-audit status/message.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "A new file, `.config/security-exceptions.toml`, holds zero or more `[[exception]]` tables, each carrying exactly these fields: `id` (string, unique within the file), `advisory_id` (string -- a `RUSTSEC-YYYY-NNNN` ID, or, for a yanked-package finding with no RUSTSEC ID, the literal form `\"yanked:<crate>@<version>\"`, e.g. `\"yanked:bisync@0.3.0\"`), `dependency_path` (string -- the dependency chain as printed by `cargo tree -i <crate> --workspace --prefix none`, arrows rendered as `\" <- \"`, e.g. `\"bisync <- gix-protocol <- gix <- callisto-vcs <- callisto-cli\"`), `owner` (string, non-empty, a GitHub username or team handle), `tracking_issue` (string, non-empty, a full `https://` GitHub issue URL), `created` (string, `YYYY-MM-DD`), `expires` (string, `YYYY-MM-DD`), `production_reachable` (boolean), and `approved_by` (string; non-empty required when `production_reachable = true`, may be `\"\"` when `production_reachable = false`). This schema is the single source of truth for every field named in AC-004 and AC-005 -- neither criterion introduces any field not listed here. This requirement governs exceptions created or renewed after this spec ships (see non_goals for the pre-existing 17 deny.toml ignores).",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The audit tool parses `.config/security-exceptions.toml` (schema per AC-003) and exits with code `4`, printing one line per violation naming the exception `id` and the specific rule broken, when any of: (a) an exception's `expires` date is in the past relative to the audit run's UTC date; (b) an exception's `expires` date is more than 90 days after its `created` date; (c) an exception's `dependency_path` does not match any path in the current `cargo tree -i <crate-from-advisory_id> --workspace --prefix none` output for the crate named in its `advisory_id` -- this includes both the crate being entirely absent from the tree and the crate still being present but reachable only through a different intermediate chain than the recorded `dependency_path` (e.g. after `cargo update` reroutes it through a new intermediate crate); or (d) `production_reachable = true` and `approved_by = \"\"`. Exit code `4` is exclusive to this criterion -- distinct from `0`, `1`, `2` (AC-007), and `3` (AC-002).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "`.config/security-exceptions.toml` contains an exception record for the transitive bisync yanked-package finding (`bisync 0.3.0`, pulled in via `gix-protocol 0.64.0 -> gix 0.86.0 -> callisto-vcs -> {callisto-cli, callisto-graph}`; as of this spec, `gix-protocol` 0.65.1, the latest published release, still requires `bisync`, so no dependency-update resolution is available and this criterion is exception-only), with `advisory_id = \"yanked:bisync@0.3.0\"` and `production_reachable = true`. `production_reachable` is determined mechanically, not by judgment call: run `cargo metadata --all-features --format-version=1`, and for the crate in question, `production_reachable = true` if and only if at least one path from any workspace member's root package to that crate in the `resolve.nodes[].deps[]` graph consists entirely of edges whose `dep_kinds[].kind` is `\"normal\"` or `\"build\"` (no `\"dev\"`-kind edge anywhere on that path); `false` only if every such path contains at least one `\"dev\"`-kind edge. For bisync, the `callisto-vcs -> callisto-cli` and `callisto-vcs -> callisto-graph` paths are both `\"normal\"`-kind throughout, so `production_reachable = true` and (per AC-003's schema) `approved_by` must be non-empty. `SECURITY.md` (which already documents `just audit` under its Scope section) gains a row in a new `## Tracked Exceptions` section listing this exception's `id`, `owner`, and `expires` date, kept in sync with `.config/security-exceptions.toml`.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Locally, `just audit` invokes `moon run :audit`, which per `.moon/tasks/rust.yml` runs plain `cargo-deny check advisories` (human-readable output, no `-f json`) -- this remains unchanged by this spec and is what a developer runs locally. In CI, the `security` job in `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml` runs `just audit` (identical to local) AND, as an additional step, runs the audit tool from AC-001 (which internally invokes `cargo deny -f json check advisories`), writing `target/security-audit-record.json` (AC-001) and a second file `target/security-audit-report.json` containing the raw `cargo deny -f json check advisories` stdout. Both files are uploaded as CI artifacts via a single `actions/upload-artifact@v4` step (name `security-audit-artifacts`, path `target/security-audit-record.json` and `target/security-audit-report.json`) added to the `security` job, matching the existing `actions/upload-artifact@v4` usage in the `coverage` job.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "When `rustsec::Database::fetch()` succeeds but the returned `Database`'s `.latest_commit()` (`git` feature) returns `None` -- e.g. because the fetched advisory-db is not a git checkout -- the audit tool exits with code `2` and prints a message containing the literal substring `advisory database revision could not be determined: not a git checkout`. It does not write `target/security-audit-record.json`, and it never emits a record with an absent, empty, or placeholder `advisory_db_commit_id` field. This is distinct from AC-002's exit code `3` (fetch/update itself failing) -- here the fetch succeeded but no git revision exists to record.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-SUPPLY-CHAIN-REPRODUCIBILITY",
  "implementation_notes": [
    "`rustsec` (0.33.0, `git` feature) is NOT currently a dependency anywhere in this workspace's Cargo.lock (`cargo tree -i rustsec` fails to match) -- it must be added as a new direct dependency of whatever crate hosts the audit tool, not assumed to already be present transitively via cargo-deny/cargo-audit.",
    "Verified locally: `cargo deny --version` -> `cargo-deny 0.19.9`. `-f`/`--format` (human|json|sarif) is a GLOBAL flag that must precede the `check` subcommand: `cargo deny -f json check advisories` succeeds; `cargo deny check advisories -f json` fails with `error: unexpected argument '-f' found`.",
    "Verified by reading `deny.toml`: the `[advisories] ignore` array currently has 17 entries (13 wasmtime RUSTSEC IDs + RUSTSEC-2024-0370 proc-macro-error + 3 im-rc/sized-chunks/bitmaps IDs), all dev-only via `moon_pdk_test_utils`/`warpgate`/`wasmtime`/`wasm-compose` per the file's own comment block -- not 11. This spec's exception-record store (AC-003) does not require migrating these 17; see non_goals.",
    "Verified via `cargo tree -i bisync`: `bisync 0.3.0` resolves through `gix-protocol 0.64.0 -> gix 0.86.0 -> callisto-vcs`, then fans out to `callisto-cli` (normal), `callisto-graph` (normal, also via `callisto-moon`), and `callisto-conventional` (dev-dependency of `callisto-cli`) -- at least one path (`callisto-vcs -> callisto-cli`) is normal-kind throughout, so AC-005's mechanical `production_reachable` determination correctly yields `true`.",
    "`.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml`'s `security` job already installs `cargo-deny` via `taiki-e/install-action@v2` and runs `just audit`; AC-001/AC-006's new steps are additions to that existing job, not a new job. The `coverage` job already demonstrates the `actions/upload-artifact@v4` pattern this spec reuses.",
    "`SECURITY.md` already exists at the repo root and already references `just audit` (`cargo deny check advisories`) under its Scope section -- AC-005 extends this existing file with a new section rather than inventing `docs/security-advisories.md` (the flat `docs/` directory has no security-focused file and no established convention for one).",
    "Workspace members are declared as `members = [\"crates/*\"]` in the root `Cargo.toml` -- any new crate hosting the audit tool must live under `crates/` to be picked up automatically; exact crate name/placement is left to the implementation plan.",
    "`.config/` already exists in this repo (currently holds only `nextest.toml`), so `.config/security-exceptions.toml` (AC-003) follows an established convention rather than introducing a new top-level directory."
  ]
}

